===== PART A: git commit -a -m  vs  git commit -m =====

--- Modify a TRACKED file, then use plain "git commit -m" (no -a) ---
$ git status --short
 M file.txt
?? part-a.log
$ git commit -m "add line2"   (forgot to git add)
On branch main
Changes not staged for commit:
  (use "git add <file>..." to update what will be committed)
  (use "git restore <file>..." to discard changes in working directory)
	modified:   file.txt

Untracked files:
  (use "git add <file>..." to include in what will be committed)
	part-a.log

no changes added to commit (use "git add" and/or "git commit -a")
$ git status --short   (change is still uncommitted, still shows modified)
 M file.txt
?? part-a.log

--- Now use "git commit -a -m" on the same still-modified file ---
$ git commit -a -m "add line2 (with -a)"
[main 65f33e0] add line2 (with -a)
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)
$ git status --short   (clean now)
?? part-a.log

CONCLUSION: -a automatically stages changes to files git ALREADY tracks
(modified/deleted), skipping a separate "git add". It does NOT stage
brand-new untracked files - those still need an explicit "git add".
Plain "git commit -m" only commits what is already in the staging area.
